//? Title: Sequential probability ratios and confidence sequences
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

For two simple Bernoulli hypotheses, each event contributes a log
likelihood ratio. Wald's rule compares the cumulative sum with two
stopping boundaries. The first boundary reached determines the decision.

The confidence sequence for a bounded mean controls the probability of
missing a constant conditional mean at any observation count.

```{cpp, id=sequential-inference, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/sequential.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    SequentialProbabilityRatioTest sprt(.05, .2);
    std::println("The log likelihood stopping boundaries are {:.3f} and {:.3f}.",
                 sprt.lower_boundary(), sprt.upper_boundary());
    for (int i = 1; i <= 8; ++i) {
        const auto decision = sprt.observe(std::log(.75 / .5));
        if (decision == SequentialDecision::AcceptAlternative) {
            std::println("After {} successes, the log likelihood ratio is {:.3f} and the test accepts p = 0.75.",
                         i, sprt.log_likelihood_ratio());
            break;
        }
    }

    BoundedMeanConfidenceSequence sequence(0, 1);
    std::println("\n| Observations | Mean | Lower | Upper |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (int i = 1; i <= 128; ++i) {
        const auto interval = sequence.observe(i % 2);
        if (i == 8 || i == 32 || i == 128)
            std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",
                         i, *interval.estimate, interval.lower, interval.upper);
    }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
The log likelihood stopping boundaries are -1.558 and 2.773.
After 7 successes, the log likelihood ratio is 2.838 and the test accepts p = 0.75.

| Observations | Mean | Lower | Upper |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 8 | 0.500 | 0.000 | 1.000 |
| 32 | 0.500 | 0.232 | 0.768 |
| 128 | 0.500 | 0.362 | 0.638 |
